<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Mohammadgarh State</h1><p class="st-meta">1818 CE – 1948 CE (approx.) · capital Mohammadgarh</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small Muslim-ruled state of the Bhopal Agency in central India, a branch of the Pathan family of Kurwai.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Mohammadgarh was separated from the lands of the Pathan Nawabs of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kurwai-state">Kurwai</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/basoda-state">Basoda</a> in the early 19th century and recognised as a distinct holding under British supervision around 1818.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its Nawab ruled a few villages within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhopal-agency">Bhopal Agency</a>. Little else is recorded of its history.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After accession to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> Mohammadgarh was merged into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/madhya-bharat">Madhya Bharat</a> in 1948 and joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a> in 1956.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/mohammadgarh-state.jpg" alt="Mohammadgarh State" width="320" height="197"><figcaption>Image: Great Britain India Office · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Basoda-Muhammadgarh_map.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1818 CE – 1948 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mohammadgarh</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism; Sunni Islam (ruling house)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hindi</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>India</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Muhammadgarh</dd></dl></aside></div>
